WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.450
Last week I played a joke

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:03.630
on my little nephew.

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.400
I showed him a lemon and said “banana”.

4
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.090
The next day, he called

5
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.770
every lemon a banana!

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.920
Could a computer be fooled like that too?

7
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.690
Today: how AI learns from examples, and

8
00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.690
what happens when some of them are wrong.

9
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.970
To teach a computer to sort fruit,

10
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.050
we show it lots of pictures.

11
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.300
Each one has a label: “mango” or “lemon”.

12
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.360
The computer finds what each

13
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.560
kind has in common.

14
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.840
Then it sorts new fruit by itself.

15
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.460
Those labels are its teacher.

16
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.300
If the labels are right, it learns well.

17
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.360
But if some labels are wrong, it

18
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.960
learns wrong things too.

19
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.840
It can't tell that a label is a mistake.

20
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.070
Mistakes can happen by accident.

21
00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.710
But someone could also add wrong

22
00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.290
examples on purpose, to spoil the AI.

23
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.700
Grown-ups call this “poisoning” the data.

24
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:05.330
Quick check!

25
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.890
Someone sneaks ten wrong labels

26
00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.090
into the fruit pictures.

27
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.970
What happens when the

28
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.230
computer learns from them?

29
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.520
It makes more mistakes.

30
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.390
It learned from every label,

31
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:18.790
the wrong ones too.

32
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.750
Now some lemons get sorted as mangoes.

33
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.910
How can we find the wrong examples?

34
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.270
Look for the odd ones out.

35
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.680
A “lemon” that is big and very sweet

36
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.780
sits right among the mangoes.

37
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.190
That label is probably wrong!

38
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.000
People who build AI check where

39
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.200
their examples came from.

40
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.310
They look for odd ones out.

41
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.200
Then they fix them or take them away.

42
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.240
In the app, slide up the wrong

43
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.060
labels and press Train.

44
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.880
Watch the line move and the score drop.

45
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.540
Then press “Find the odd

46
00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:58.820
ones out” to catch them!

47
00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:00.510
Your turn.

48
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.600
Your class is teaching a

49
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.260
computer to sort leaves.

50
00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.860
A friend labels a few leaves

51
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.220
wrongly, as a joke.

52
00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.630
What should you do?

53
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.840
Fix them, and tell your teacher.

54
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.450
Remember: AI learns from

55
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.690
examples with labels.

56
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.180
Wrong labels teach it wrong answers.

57
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.600
And looking for odd ones out

58
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.840
helps us find them.

59
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.180
Try it tomorrow: play “Teach the sorter”.

60
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.130
One friend is the computer, and learns

61
00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:32.490
from picture cards with labels.

62
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.880
Sneak in one wrong label, and see

63
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.220
what the “computer” learns!
